{
    "word": "reification",
    "lang_code": "en",
    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "9e668228-6082-321b-80e8-c9d57076e11f",
                "word": "reification",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "83b24cea-8f94-4133-b96d-faec0e951f8f",
                        "entry_id": "9e668228-6082-321b-80e8-c9d57076e11f",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "reifications"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "bbde87b5-f53c-3717-a287-d90d3d037e8c",
                        "entry_id": "9e668228-6082-321b-80e8-c9d57076e11f",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The consideration of an abstract thing as if it were concrete, or of an inanimate object as if it were living."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "791c79aa-926e-45e9-b1f2-de7d01814e77",
                                "sense_id": "bbde87b5-f53c-3717-a287-d90d3d037e8c",
                                "text": "The reification of art and religion, a symptom of their historical obsolescence, takes the form of their instrumentalization, their reduction to a mere use value. At this point they become ‘cultural goods’, writes Adorno, and ‘are no longer taken quite seriously by anybody.’"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "71b484d7-c46f-4340-907f-258db68e2720",
                                "sense_id": "bbde87b5-f53c-3717-a287-d90d3d037e8c",
                                "text": "Computer scientists found out how functional reification is in programming languages: they call it ‘object-oriented programming’ […]."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0f33e5cd-b7cb-468f-af0a-c4021343f8d7",
                                "sense_id": "bbde87b5-f53c-3717-a287-d90d3d037e8c",
                                "text": "Reification swaps out a political problem for a scientific or technical one; it’s how, for example, the effects of unregulated tech oligopolies become “social media addiction,” how climate catastrophe caused by corporate greed becomes a “heat wave” — and, by the way, how the effect of struggles between labor and corporations combines with high energy prices to become “inflation.”"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "75e419d6-5928-3f36-802c-c68008fc6209",
                        "entry_id": "9e668228-6082-321b-80e8-c9d57076e11f",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The consideration of a human being as an impersonal object."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "64f4e300-b2a8-3daf-b94b-6139fdde53a5",
                        "entry_id": "9e668228-6082-321b-80e8-c9d57076e11f",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A process that makes a computable/addressable object out of a non-computable/addressable one; or a concrete class out of a generic one."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "acdbb7d7-21f6-4a07-ab93-4109e1f2551d",
                                "sense_id": "64f4e300-b2a8-3daf-b94b-6139fdde53a5",
                                "text": "Contrary to Java, C++ and C# implement generics via reification, meaning that each specific version of a generic class, like List<String> is converted into a concrete class, either at compile time (C++) or at runtime (C#)."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "271a1c8d-18d6-30b3-98ef-0033a101c70f",
                        "entry_id": "9e668228-6082-321b-80e8-c9d57076e11f",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The transformation of a natural-language statement into a form in which its actions and events are quantifiable variables."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "b2235218-f1c2-41a1-9fd9-838f21daf5d6",
                        "source_entry_id": "9e668228-6082-321b-80e8-c9d57076e11f",
                        "source_sense_description": "Consideration of an abstract thing as if it were concrete, or of an inanimate object as if it were living",
                        "target_lang_word": "réification",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "réification",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "abdb7868-8656-322e-819b-e38cb91a621b",
                                        "entry_id": "b3f5a23f-cdad-304c-9964-b195c37682f2",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Fait de transformer en chose, ce qui est une idée, un mouvement, un concept."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "834a2dc4-3eba-4f96-848e-0f13577b518f",
                                                "sense_id": "abdb7868-8656-322e-819b-e38cb91a621b",
                                                "text": "La réification est l’appréhension d’un phénomène humain en tant que chose, c’est-à-dire en des termes non-humains ou même supra-humains."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "30b2928c-8985-47fe-8d3c-d7c0c83e1fde",
                                                "sense_id": "abdb7868-8656-322e-819b-e38cb91a621b",
                                                "text": "S’en suivra en toute logique une intensification égale de la réification ; le monde, la nature et les êtres vivants n’étant voués à être que des systèmes émetteurs de signaux interprétables par une machine, des sources de données rentables."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "04e20927-62c8-42ee-945f-8496bcffc299",
